---
import GuideLayout from '../../layouts/GuideLayout.astro';
import { guides, site, sitePath } from '../../data/site';
import image from '../../../../screenshots/text-expansion-tab.png';

const toc = [
  { id: 'what-it-does', label: 'What text expansion does' },
  { id: 'before-you-start', label: 'Before you start' },
  { id: 'first-expansion', label: 'Create your first expansion' },
  { id: 'insertion-mode', label: 'Choose an insertion mode' },
  { id: 'questions', label: 'Common questions' },
];
---

<GuideLayout
  guide={guides.textExpansion}
  image={image}
  imageAlt="CrossMacro Text Expansion tab showing expansion rules and insertion methods"
  toc={toc}
>
  <section id="what-it-does">
    <h2>What text expansion does</h2>
    <p>
      CrossMacro text expansion replaces a short typed trigger with saved text on Linux,
      Windows, and macOS. For example, typing <code>:mail</code> can insert
      <code>hello@example.com</code>. Use it for non-sensitive email addresses, signatures,
      repeated replies, or boilerplate you would otherwise retype.
    </p>
    <p>
      This is different from a full mouse and keyboard macro. An expansion inserts text;
      a recorded macro replays a sequence of input actions, such as navigating an interface.
      For that workflow, start with the
      <a href={sitePath(guides.wayland.route)}>Linux and Wayland macro recording guide</a>.
      Neither approach guarantees that every target application accepts automated input.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section id="before-you-start">
    <h2>Before you start</h2>
    <p>
      <a href={`${sitePath('/')}#download`}>Install CrossMacro for your platform</a> and keep
      its desktop runtime running in an active desktop session. Complete the relevant
      input setup before creating rules:
    </p>
    <ul>
      <li>
        <strong>Linux:</strong> follow the
        <a href={`${site.repository}/blob/dev/docs/linux.md`}>Linux setup reference</a>
        for your package. Native daemon-backed packages and portable Wayland packages
        use different paths. Flatpak and AppImage on Wayland need readable input event
        devices and writable uinput; clipboard access alone is insufficient.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>macOS:</strong> macOS 14 or newer requires Input Monitoring for capture
        and Accessibility approval for insertion. Follow the
        <a href={`${site.repository}/blob/dev/docs/macos.md#grant-permissions`}>macOS privacy instructions</a>.
        Ordinary text expansion does not require Screen Recording permission.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Windows:</strong> follow the
        <a href={`${site.repository}/blob/dev/docs/windows.md`}>Windows installation and desktop-session guide</a>.
        Normal input use has no CrossMacro-specific Windows privacy prompt, but a target
        application can still reject an operation.
      </li>
    </ul>
  </section>

  <section id="first-expansion">
    <h2>Create your first expansion</h2>
    <ol>
      <li>In Settings, turn on <strong>Enable Text Expansion</strong>.</li>
      <li>
        Open Text Expansion and enter <code>:mail</code> as the trigger and
        <code>hello@example.com</code> as the replacement.
      </li>
      <li>Choose an insertion mode, add the entry, and leave that individual entry enabled.</li>
      <li>
        Keep CrossMacro running, focus a disposable plain-text document, and type
        <code>:mail</code> using exactly the same case.
      </li>
      <li>
        Check that the trigger is replaced by the email address. Repeat the check in
        your intended application before relying on the rule.
      </li>
    </ol>
    <p>
      Expansion happens as soon as the typed buffer ends with an enabled trigger.
      <strong>No Space or Enter is required.</strong> Matching is case-sensitive and does
      not require a word boundary. Choose short, distinctive triggers, such as a colon
      followed by a word, and avoid overlapping triggers or ordinary words that could
      expand unexpectedly. Type the trigger for testing; do not assume pasting it activates a rule.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section id="insertion-mode">
    <h2>Choose an insertion mode</h2>
    <p>
      <strong>Paste</strong> inserts replacement text through the clipboard. CrossMacro
      attempts to restore clipboard contents afterward, but restoration is best-effort
      and does not guarantee preservation of every clipboard format. Choose a paste
      method appropriate for the target application.
    </p>
    <p>
      <strong>Direct typing</strong> inserts through simulated typing instead of clipboard
      paste. It offers fast batched and compatible key-by-key methods. Test your chosen
      method with the target application and keyboard layout; neither mode promises
      universal compatibility. Start with a short, harmless replacement so incorrect
      insertion is easy to spot and discard.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section id="questions">
    <h2>Common questions</h2>
    <h3>Why does my saved expansion not run?</h3>
    <p>
      Check both the global Settings switch and the entry's enabled state, then the
      trigger's exact case, active profile, running desktop runtime, and input permissions.
      Profiles have their own expansion settings and entries.
    </p>
    <h3>Does expansion work after I exit CrossMacro?</h3>
    <p>
      No. Fully exiting the runtime stops expansion. Hiding a tray-enabled window can
      leave it running. The
      <a href={`${site.repository}/blob/dev/docs/cli.md#gui-less-desktop-runtime`}>GUI-less desktop runtime</a>
      is another option, but it still requires an active desktop session.
    </p>
    <h3>Can I manage expansions from the command line?</h3>
    <p>
      Yes; see the
      <a href={`${site.repository}/blob/dev/docs/cli.md#text-expansion-command`}>text-expansion CLI reference</a>.
      Its test command resolves and reports a replacement without typing into the
      desktop. Use a harmless editor to verify actual insertion.
    </p>
  </section>
</GuideLayout>
